%[text] # **Quickly apply gravity loads and obtain mass and stiffness matrices**
%[text] A structural model is brought to its gravity equilibrium state before the assembled mass, damping, and tangent-stiffness matrices are requested. The matrix dimensions and equation ordering should be checked before using them outside OpenSees.
%[text] **This demonstrates how to quickly create gravity loads based on the mass matrix.**
%[text] **It also demonstrates how to obtain the model's stiffness, mass, and damping matrices.**
clc; clear;

opsMAT = OpenSeesMatlab();
ops = opsMAT.opensees;
%[text] ### FE Model
ops.wipe()
ops.model("basic", "-ndm", 2, "-ndf", 3)

% Define the model nodes
ops.node(1, 0.0, 0.0)
ops.node(2, 2.0, 0.0)
ops.node(3, 0.0, 2.0)
ops.node(4, 0.0, 2.0)
ops.node(5, 2.0, 2.0)
ops.node(6, 2.0, 2.0)

% Constrain the nodes
ops.rigidLink("beam", 3, 4)  % Rigid link between nodes 3 and 4
ops.equalDOF(5, 6, 1, 2, 3)  % Equal DOF between nodes 5 and 6

ops.fix(1, 1, 1, 1)
ops.fix(2, 1, 1, 1)

% Assign the node masses
ops.mass(1, 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)
ops.mass(2, 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)
ops.mass(3, 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)
ops.mass(4, 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)
ops.mass(5, 10.0, 10.0, 1e-3)
ops.mass(6, 10.0, 10.0, 1e-3)

% Define the elements
ops.geomTransf("Linear", 1)

% element('elasticBeamColumn', eleTag, *eleNodes, Area, E_mod, Iz, transfTag, <'-mass', mass>)
Area = 1.0;
E_mod = 1.0;
Iz = 1.0;
ops.element("elasticBeamColumn", 1, 1, 3, Area, E_mod, Iz, 1, "-mass", 1.0)
ops.element("elasticBeamColumn", 2, 2, 5, Area, E_mod, Iz, 1, "-mass", 1.0)
ops.element("elasticBeamColumn", 3, 4, 6, Area, E_mod, Iz, 1, "-mass", 1.0)
%[text] ### Apply the gravity load
%[text] The opstool utility provides a function `pre.createGravityLoad` to quickly apply gravity loads. **This function internally retrieves all masses in the model, including those defined at nodes, in elements, and in materials.** You only need to provide the multiplier (gravitational acceleration) and the direction of load application.
ops.timeSeries("Constant", 1)  % Define a constant time series, tag = 1
ops.pattern("Plain", 1, 1)  % Define a load pattern, tag = 1
node_loads = opsMAT.pre.createGravityLoad(direction="Y", factor=-9.81);  % Create gravity loads in the pattern tag=1
display(node_loads); %[output:52967986]
figure;
opts = opsMAT.vis.defaultPlotModelOptions;
opts.loads.showNodal = true;
opsMAT.vis.plotModel(opts=opts); %[output:8f716365] %[output:2a8d2722]
%[text] 🤖 Therefore, you can easily create gravity loads directly through the above commands. The way you specify the mass when modeling is arbitrary. opstool can help you do all the internal conversions!
%[text] ### Get Model Mass
%[text] `pre.getNodeMass` returns a dictionary containing all masses defined in the model, both at nodes and elements.
node_mass = opsMAT.pre.getNodeMass();

ks = keys(node_mass);
vs = values(node_mass);
for i = 1:numel(ks) %[output:group:192e6c5b]
    fprintf('Key: '); %[output:837d8184] %[output:928fb957] %[output:4e420168] %[output:65da5939] %[output:7c152628] %[output:0b741a29]
    disp(ks{i}); %[output:3559e86e] %[output:399f37ed] %[output:7a79b4f9] %[output:234e3a93] %[output:60dd1a2c] %[output:49a67e33]

    fprintf('Value: '); %[output:957aa12d] %[output:5cae2d89] %[output:8737a5f5] %[output:9a1e6993] %[output:8d824e53] %[output:1efcb4a5]
    disp(vs{i}); %[output:87ed01f5] %[output:6293e74e] %[output:9e2b3f24] %[output:4f6ddbb0] %[output:317b9fc6] %[output:3d181978]
end %[output:group:192e6c5b]
%[text] ### Get system matrix
%[text] #### **Get Matrix from Penalty-constraint method**
constraints_args = {"Penalty", 1e10, 1e10};  % This affects the dimensions of the matrix
system_args = {"FullGeneral"}; % Full matrix system must be used
numberer_args = {"RCM"};  % It affects the topology of the matrix and the order of dof.

M = opsMAT.pre.getMCK("m", constraintsArgs=constraints_args, systemArgs=system_args, numbererArgs=numberer_args);
K = opsMAT.pre.getMCK("k", constraintsArgs=constraints_args, systemArgs=system_args, numbererArgs=numberer_args);
M.Data %[output:20841aa3]
M.Labels  % nodeTag-dof %[output:8a06ade9]
figure; %[output:91f98bd3]
imagesc(M.Data); %[output:91f98bd3]
axis equal tight; %[output:91f98bd3]
colormap(parula);   % turbo, jet ... %[output:91f98bd3]
colorbar; %[output:91f98bd3]

xticks(1:numel(M.Labels)); %[output:91f98bd3]
yticks(1:numel(M.Labels)); %[output:91f98bd3]
xticklabels(M.Labels); %[output:91f98bd3]
xtickangle(90); %[output:91f98bd3]
yticklabels(M.Labels); %[output:91f98bd3]

title('Mass Matrix Visualization'); %[output:91f98bd3]
%[text] It can be observed that all degrees of freedom are preserved in the system matrix, and a large penalty number is added.
%[text] #### **Get Matrix from Transformation-constraint approach**
constraints_args = {"Transformation"};  % This affects the dimensions of the matrix
system_args = {"FullGeneral"};  % Full matrix system must be used
numberer_args = {"Plain"};  % It affects the topology of the matrix and the order of dof.

M = opsMAT.pre.getMCK("m", constraintsArgs=constraints_args, systemArgs=system_args, numbererArgs=numberer_args);
K = opsMAT.pre.getMCK("k", constraintsArgs=constraints_args, systemArgs=system_args, numbererArgs=numberer_args);

figure; %[output:2ccb61e9]
imagesc(K.Data); %[output:2ccb61e9]
axis equal tight; %[output:2ccb61e9]
colormap(parula);   % turbo, jet ... %[output:2ccb61e9]
colorbar; %[output:2ccb61e9]

xticks(1:numel(K.Labels)); %[output:2ccb61e9]
yticks(1:numel(K.Labels)); %[output:2ccb61e9]
xticklabels(K.Labels); %[output:2ccb61e9]
xtickangle(90); %[output:2ccb61e9]
yticklabels(K.Labels); %[output:2ccb61e9]

title('Stiffness Matrix Visualization'); %[output:2ccb61e9]
%[text] It can be observed that the constrained degrees of freedom are eliminated from the system matrix (only nodes 4 and 6 are retained), thus reducing the dimension of the system matrix.
%[text] ### Checking assembled matrices
%[text] Matrix sizes must match the active equation count. Check expected symmetry and sparsity, and remember that the tangent stiffness depends on the current committed model state.

